DOWNLOAD SPEED INVESTIGATION - WHAT WORKED AND WHAT DID NOT
==========================================================
Time : 2026-10-20 23:31

QUESTION
  The uncensored Qwen-Image set was downloading at ~208-233 KB/s (9.6 GB = 11-12 h). Is there a faster
  mirror?

MIRROR SURVEY (measured, not guessed)
  hf-mirror.com            206  348 KB/s   <- baseline, also the best
  ai.gitcode.com hf_mirrors 200 341 KB/s   <- works, but no faster than baseline
  gitcode.com/hf_mirrors   206   91 KB/s
  hf-api.gitee.com         404
  modelscope.cn            fast, but hosts ONLY the censored official Qwen-Image (uncensored repos 404)
  huggingface.co           ECONNRESET (blocked)
  Conclusion: ~350 KB/s is the ceiling of this network path, not a mirror limitation. No faster source
  exists for these specific weights.

PARALLEL DOWNLOAD ATTEMPTS (both failed, worth recording so nobody repeats it)
  v2 segmented downloader: split each file into 8 ranges and fetch them concurrently.
    - Benchmark said 8 concurrent ranges reached 341 KB/s vs 208 single, so the idea looked sound.
    - In practice hf-mirror HANGS on 8 concurrent RANGE requests over large spans: the requests open,
      return 206, and then deliver nothing. All eight .segN files stayed at 0 bytes across two runs.
    - I first suspected an event race (close firing before end, resolving the promise early and
      truncating the write). Fixed that - behaviour unchanged. The race was real but not the cause;
      the stall is server-side.
    - Verdict: hf-mirror tolerates small concurrent probes but not 8 large concurrent range pulls.

A SELF-INFLICTED PROBLEM WORTH REMEMBERING
  job_kill terminates the pwsh wrapper, NOT the node child process it spawned. After "killing" the v2
  job, the node process kept running alongside the v1 downloader, splitting the bandwidth (measured
  183 KB/s instead of ~350). The symptom was a .part file growing slower than the log claimed.
  Fix: enumerate node processes by command line and Stop-Process them explicitly:
      Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter "Name='node.exe'" |
        Where-Object { $_.CommandLine -match 'download-qwen-image' } | Stop-Process -Force
  Rule of thumb: after killing any job that spawned a long-lived child, verify with a process listing.

ALSO MISLEADING
  PowerShell's (Get-Item).Length lagged behind reality for a file being appended by node; node's own
  fs.statSync showed the true size (18.5 MB while PowerShell said 0). Trust node's stat when auditing
  an active download.

FINAL CONFIGURATION
  One single-connection v1 downloader, sequential: VAE 0.63 GB -> diffusion 4.29 GB -> encoder 4.68 GB,
  resuming from the retained .part. Expect roughly 9-10 hours at ~300 KB/s. Nothing else is running.